# Every `docker build` sends its ENTIRE context directory to the Docker
# daemon before the build even starts, regardless of what a Dockerfile's
# COPY instructions actually use — this file is what keeps that context
# small and stale-artifact-free. Two concrete costs an empty
# .dockerignore would have here: target/ directories across 8 modules
# (locally-built .class files and jars we don't want silently reused
# inside a container image meant to be built FRESH from source) and
# .git/ (this repo's entire commit history, genuinely large, and never
# needed inside a build).

target/
.git/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
.DS_Store
.claude/
cloud/cdk/node_modules/
cloud/cdk/cdk.out/
